x86_64-linux  The closure type's own name, and nothing else in this case. [expr.prim.lambda] makes a closure a unique unnamed type, so there is no name either compiler is obliged to match: cxx1 spells its display tag - `NK3$_0clEv` - where clang writes the Itanium closure encoding `NKUlvE_clEv`. All three call operators differ this way and every other name in the file agrees. Nothing links against these: a closure's call operator is reached through the object, and the object never leaves the function that made it.
x86_64-windows  The same one difference, plus the half this ABI adds: clang spells the closure `<lambda_1>` at scope `?0` where cxx1 writes `$_0` at `?1`, and clang encodes the deduced return type as `@` where cxx1 writes the type it deduced - `??R<lambda_1>@?0??readsThrough@S@@QEBAHXZ@QEBA@XZ` against `??R$_0@?1??readsThrough@S@@QEBAHXZ@QEBAHXZ`. Both are recorded in CLAUDE.md for the closures that came before this case. Every other name agrees.
arm64-darwin  The same closure-name difference as x86_64-linux, with Mach-O's leading underscore: `__ZZNK1S12readsThroughEvENK3$_0clEv` against clang's `__ZZNK1S12readsThroughEvENKUlvE_clEv`. Every other name in the case agrees.
